# Fast decimal-string -> nat.
#
# Horner's rule turns each digit into acc := 10*acc + digit, and what a literal
# costs is dominated by walking the accumulator's bits once per digit — the
# quadratic term of parsing a literal. Taking digits in *pairs* instead,
# acc := 100*acc + (10*d1 + d2), halves how many times the accumulator is
# walked, and that is the whole game: the per-block digit arithmetic (three short
# table lookups) is noise next to a walk of the accumulator.
#
# The walk itself carries the value still owed at the current position: the
# amount pending at a bit is [carry + 100*bit], of which the low bit is the
# answer there and the rest carries into the next position. That carry never
# exceeds 100 — the fixed point of c -> (c + 100) / 2, entered at a digit pair,
# which is at most 99 — so it fits in seven bits, and the whole per-carry step
# lives in a table small enough to state exhaustively.
#
# Emitting the leftover carry as the high end is what keeps the result
# canonical: the top bit of a canonical accumulator is set, so the carry leaving
# it is at least 50, and an accumulator of zero emits the digit pair alone.
#
# An odd-length literal starts with a lone digit: a cheap parity pre-pass
# decides whether the first block is one digit or two, so the accumulator is
# seeded before the paired fold and no single-digit step exists at all.
#
# A [Memo] table over small *accumulators* used to sit in front of the walk,
# turning every step of a 1- or 2-digit literal into a single lookup; it was
# removed because ~167k nodes of build per module load cost more than the walk
# it saved. Don't bring it back. The per-carry table here is different: carries
# are bounded by the radix whatever the literal, and its entries share their
# structure, so both tables together add ~0.1% to the compiled bundle while the
# recorded [size] (which unfolds that sharing) grows much more.
#
# Non-digit characters below 64 contribute their [Nat.sub_x_48] value, chars
# >= 64 contribute 0 — callers only pass digit characters.
#
# This is the [__fastest] variant (the smaller default [from_string] lives in
# nat.lamb). It lives in its own file rather than nat.lamb because it depends on
# [Memo], and [Memo] in turn depends on core [Nat] (e.g. [Nat.pred] in
# [gen_list]). Keeping it separate makes the file-level dependency graph acyclic
# (nat -> memo -> from_string) instead of forming a Nat <-> Memo cycle.
_decimal_digit = Memo.precompute_alt Memo.gen_ascii_low 0 Nat.sub_x_48
# A block's value 10*d1 + d2 comes out of a 16x16 table keyed by the two
# digits' bit lists — an [add_const]-chain adder was measured at 116-220
# steps depending on the digit, while two digit conversions and a short trie
# walk cost ~50, less for small digits whose keys are shorter.
_pair_val_tab = Memo.precompute_multi [Memo.gen_list Memo.gen_bool 4; Memo.gen_list Memo.gen_bool 4] (\d1 \d2 Nat.add (Nat.mul_const 10 d1) d2)
_pair_val = \c1 \c2 _pair_val_tab (_decimal_digit c1) (_decimal_digit c2)
_gen_carry = Memo.gen_list Memo.gen_bool 7
# The table entry for a carry IS the loop body for that carry, and it consumes
# THREE accumulator bits per iteration as one prebuilt triage tree: the cons
# dispatch and the bit dispatch are stored eliminator values whose cases are
# the next level down, so a bit costs two native triage rules and two
# applications — nothing is matched with a combinator built on the spot, and
# no head, div2 or output cons is ever computed at run time. Every output-bit
# spine, follow-on carry and eliminator sits in the entry as a constant; the
# carry-trie lookup is paid at most once per three bits — see the chained
# table below. A level's value takes
# (tail, self): triage on the tail either finishes on the exhausted
# accumulator (absorbing self) or lands the bit eliminator on the head bit,
# whose selected case takes the remaining tail and the knot.
_hp_entry = \leaf \c
    let (Nat.add c 100) $ \cT
    let (List.match false (\hd \_ hd) c)  $ \o_F
    let (List.match false (\hd \_ hd) cT) $ \o_T
    let (Nat.div2 c)  $ \m_F
    let (Nat.div2 cT) $ \m_T
    let (Nat.add m_F 100) $ \sFT
    let (Nat.add m_T 100) $ \sTT
    # [tail2 o0 o1 m2] is the level that has consumed two bits producing o0
    # and o1, with m2 pending.
    let (\o0 \o1 \m2
        let (Nat.add m2 100) $ \s2
        let (List.match false (\hd \_ hd) m2) $ \h2F
        let (List.match false (\hd \_ hd) s2) $ \h2T
        let (Nat.div2 m2) $ \m3F
        let (Nat.div2 s2) $ \m3T
        let (\r △ o0 (△ o1 (△ h2F r))) $ \outF
        let (\r △ o0 (△ o1 (△ h2T r))) $ \outT
        triage (\_self △ o0 (△ o1 m2))
               _
               (triage (leaf outF m3F)
                       (\_ leaf outT m3T)
                       _)) $ \tail2
    let (tail2 o_F (List.match false (\hd \_ hd) m_F) (Nat.div2 m_F)) $ \t_FF
    let (tail2 o_F (List.match false (\hd \_ hd) sFT) (Nat.div2 sFT)) $ \t_FT
    let (tail2 o_T (List.match false (\hd \_ hd) m_T) (Nat.div2 m_T)) $ \t_TF
    let (tail2 o_T (List.match false (\hd \_ hd) sTT) (Nat.div2 sTT)) $ \t_TT
    let (triage (\_self △ o_F m_F) _ (triage t_FF (\_ t_FT) _)) $ \lvl1F
    let (triage (\_self △ o_T m_T) _ (triage t_TF (\_ t_TT) _)) $ \lvl1T
    triage (\_self c)
           _
           (triage lvl1F (\_ lvl1T) _)
# A level's leaf continues the walk at a baked follow-on carry. The base
# table's leaves continue by looking the carry up in the table threaded as
# the knot — the chained trie itself — while the chained table's leaves embed
# the base table's entry for their follow-on carry directly, so the walk
# alternates chained and base entries and pays one lookup per SIX bits.
#
# Everything from here down references each heavy value exactly once: the
# recorded unfolded [size] of the conversion multiplies every reference, so
# knots thread the table (and [_pairs] its step) as arguments instead of
# capturing them, and the parity branches receive the fold rather than each
# embedding it. At run time an argument is a pointer like any other; in the
# unfolded tree it is the difference between the chained table appearing
# once and appearing eight times.
_hundred_plus_tab = Memo.precompute_multi [_gen_carry] (_hp_entry (\out \m \t \tab out (tab m t tab)))
_hundred_plus_tab2 = Memo.precompute_multi [_gen_carry] (_hp_entry (\out \m let (_hundred_plus_tab m) $ \nx \t \tab out (nx t tab)))
# 100 * n + carry, one pass over n's bits, low end first.
_hundred_plus = \n \carry (\tab tab carry n tab) _hundred_plus_tab2
_pair_step = \acc \c1 \c2 _hundred_plus acc (_pair_val c1 c2)
# The fold over digit pairs, as prebuilt eliminators like the walk's table
# entries: the two cons dispatches are stored triage values, the data flows
# in before the loop state, and the head character is reordered early so the
# outer branch is two lambdas and nothing is rebuilt or threaded through a
# deep body per iteration. The inner nil branch is never taken — the list is
# even-length by construction. The knot IS the outer eliminator, applied
# straight to the remaining list.
_pelim2 = triage _ _ (\c2 \r2 \c1 \self \step \acc self r2 self step (step acc c1 c2))
_pelim1 = triage (\_self \_step \acc acc) _ (\c1 \r1 _pelim2 r1 c1)
_odd_len = self_apply $ \self \l List.match
    (\_self false)
    (\_ \t \self List.match true (\_ \t2 self self t2) t)
    l self
# Both parity branches take the pair step and then the string, so only the
# selected one runs and only the dispatch in [from_string__fastest] embeds
# the step; their seeding eliminators are prebuilt like the fold's, and both
# enter the fold's eliminator directly once seeded. The even branch seeds
# the accumulator from the first digit pair — its value IS the accumulator,
# so the walk over a zero accumulator that a zero seed would cost is never
# taken — and a lone digit is just its own conversion, no fold at all.
_elim_e2 = triage (\_step 0) _ (\c2 \r2 \c1 \step _pelim1 r2 _pelim1 step (_pair_val c1 c2))
_elim_e1 = triage (\_step 0) _ (\c1 \r1 _elim_e2 r1 c1)
_elim_o2 = triage (\c1 \_step _decimal_digit c1) _ (\c2 \r2 \c1 \step _pelim1 (△ c2 r2) _pelim1 step (_decimal_digit c1))
_elim_o  = triage (\_step 0) _ (\c1 \r _elim_o2 r c1)
_from_even = \step \s _elim_e1 s step
_from_odd  = \step \s _elim_o s step
_parity = triage _from_even (\_ _from_odd) _
from_string__fastest = \s _parity (_odd_len s) _pair_step s

_to_string = Nat.to_source
from_string__fastest "0"
# = 0
from_string__fastest "42"
# = 42
from_string__fastest "255"
# = 255
from_string__fastest "65535"
# = 65535
from_string__fastest ""
# = 0
from_string__fastest "007"
# = 7
from_string__fastest "1000000"
# = 1000000

